[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-13", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 13 — the judge is now LDBC SNB Interactive over the network; first row 7.76 ops/s on the SF1 subset at loopback", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-13.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 13 - the judge is now LDBC SNB Interactive, over the network, against the audited world No.1\n\n> Owner instruction (2026-09-11): \"lcdb \u4e16\u754c no1 \u3092 bench \u306b\u3057\u3066. network over \u3067.\"\n> The judge changed before this iteration ran, so this iteration's first\n> job was to write the judge down (bench/benchmark.edn v2) and its second\n> was to produce the first row under it. No hypothesis about the transport\n> was tested; the seeds from iteration 12 (H2', bytes-bounded packs, H3)\n> are carried, not scored.\n\n## The judge (bench/benchmark.edn :ldbc-snb-interactive-network-v2)\n\n  workload   LDBC SNB Interactive v1 (spec/auditing v2.2.3 on the latest FDR)\n  metric     throughput (ops/s) at the highest scheduled rate whose run has\n             >= 95% of operations starting within 1 s of schedule (the spec's\n             95% on-time requirement) and 0 failed operations\n  network    driver and SUT are different processes joined by TCP/HTTP;\n             origins :loopback / :cross-host / :live are separate columns\n  target     audited world No.1 on 2026-09-11: Huawei Cloud Graph Engine\n             Service 3.1.0, 2025-12-01 - SF100 129,090 / SF300 139,411 /\n             SF1000 125,873 ops/s (1 node, 160 Kunpeng cores, 1280 GiB,\n             C++, 64 read threads, Read Committed). Previous holder\n             GraphScope Flex (Alibaba) 2024-05-14, SF300 131,264.\n  rungs      SF 1 3 10 30 100 300 1000; the audited table starts at 30\n  gate       zero failed ops, on-time >= 95%, answers agree with the\n             reference (Neo4j pairing in kotobase-peer.ldbc-snb-bench),\n             empty-answer share reported on every row\n\n## Generate - what was built to make a row possible\n\n  - `kotobase-peer.ldbc-snb-net`: the four served reads (IC02 IC07 IC08\n    IC09) from the SF1 subset behind a JDK HttpServer on 127.0.0.1, EDN in\n    and out, a failed answer is a 4xx/5xx and never an empty 200. Data and\n    queries are `ldbc-snb-bench`'s, reached through its vars exactly as\n    `ic09-diag` does, so the Neo4j equality gate that namespace holds\n    applies to what is served.\n  - `bench/ldbc_snb_driver.cljk` (nbb): open-loop scheduled driver. Op k\n    is scheduled at t0 + k/rate, round-robin over the mix, each op cycling\n    the OFFICIAL substitution parameters for SF1; --inflight slots are the\n    driver-thread analogue; scheduled vs actual start, latency, status and\n    row count are recorded; rates are swept upward until the 95% rule or a\n    failed op stops the sweep.\n  - The driver's refusals were each made to fire before the row was taken\n    (exit 2 in every case): dead SUT; SUT answering 500; SUT answering 200\n    with a body that is not an answer; parameters missing. The third\n    control caught a real defect on its first run - `not edn {{{` read as a\n    symbol and counted as an empty answer - fixed by requiring the answer's\n    shape (map with :rows vector and integer :n) before a 200 counts.\n\n## Rank - the first row (origin :loopback, rung SF1 subset 35,000 posts)\n\n    rate  sched  done  fail  on-time%  thru/s  delay-p95  empty  rows  ic02-p50  ic07-p50  ic08-p50  ic09-p50\n       2     40    40     0    100.00     2.0          2     22   6.3      62.8      43.2     153.3     598.8\n       4     80    80     0    100.00     3.8          1     47   5.8      34.5      20.0     244.6     710.5\n       8    160   160     0    100.00     7.8          2     83   7.0      32.6      24.8     236.4    1001.5\n      16    320   320     0     22.81    11.3       5632    139   8.2      46.1      22.3     434.7    1877.4\n\n    throughput 7.76 ops/s at rate 8   (SUT 8 threads, driver inflight 8,\n    host 10 cores / 32 GiB at load 12.6 / 19.2 / 21.1 with other agents)\n    target 139,411 ops/s -> ratio 1.8e4 (raw; SF1 is not on the audited table)\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The number is a queue, not a wire. Rate 8 passes with a start-delay\n    p95 of 2 ms; rate 16 fails with p95 5.6 s and every per-op p50 up\n    1.5-2x. Eight concurrent operations on a loaded 10-core host contend\n    for CPU, and IC09 at ~1 s p50 holds a slot for a second each time it\n    comes round (one in four ops). The HTTP hop is invisible at this\n    scale: server-ms and client latency differ by single milliseconds.\n  - Half the completed operations are EMPTY (83/160 at rate 8). The\n    subset rule keeps the 35,000 most recent posts - roughly the last\n    month of a 3-year simulation - while the official parameters carry\n    maxDate values across 2011-2012 and persons whose messages are mostly\n    outside that month. The row carries the share; it is a subset rung.\n    Two ways out, both stated: load full SF1 (attempted this iteration:\n    loads and builds, then the warm-up IC09 on a highest-degree person\n    over 3.1M messages sat in datalog.core/hash-join-rows for minutes -\n    the two-hop join materialises friend-of-friend x messages before the\n    date filter; not finished when this record was written), or a subset\n    rule that samples posts uniformly in time so official parameters land.\n  - IC08 at 236 ms p50 is two orders above its in-JVM harness p50 of\n    0.77 ms (2026-08-14 receipt) for the same Datalog. Three things differ\n    at once - official start persons instead of highest-degree ones, a\n    10x larger subset, 8 concurrent operations - and none is separated.\n    That separation is the first seed below, because a 300x that the\n    network row sees and the in-process row did not is exactly what the\n    owner asked this benchmark to expose.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: BASELINE (first row; growth is\ndefined from here). Recorded on the scoreboard with rung, origin, mix,\nempty share and load. Iteration 11's window/bfs result and iteration 12's\nrefutation keep their v1 rows and are not rescored.\n\nSeeds for iteration 14, in order:\n\n  - S1 (mechanism): run the same four reads at rate 1, inflight 1, with\n    (a) official parameters and (b) the harness's highest-degree persons,\n    on the same subset, and separate the IC08 300x into person choice /\n    subset size / concurrency. Predicted: most of it is the start person\n    (official persons have few kept messages, so IC08 scans replyOf\n    without a bound), and the fix is a planner choice, not the network.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 with IC09 bounded - push the date filter under\n    the join or bound the friend-of-friend set first - so the empty share\n    reaches ~0 and the rung is SF1 proper. The measured cost of the\n    unbounded join (minutes on one warm-up) is the reason.\n  - S3 (mix): add the short reads IS1-IS7 - point lookups the datom plane\n    already answers well (2026-07-25 receipts) - so the mix moves toward\n    the real one and the on-time rule is tested by a stream that looks\n    like LDBC's.\n  - Carried from iteration 12, unscored under v2: H2' (packs cut at\n    prolly node boundaries), bytes-bounded packs, H3 (uncontributing\n    packs). They are transport hypotheses; under v2 they matter only when\n    the SUT reads over IPLD (origin :live), which is the last origin on\n    the ladder.\n"}]
